Title: Page Breaks
Credit: Written by
Author: Andy Example

FADE IN:

INT. TOWN HALL - NIGHT

The hall smells of floor polish and wet coats. Forty folding chairs face a stage that is really just four tables pushed together under a velvet curtain the colour of old blood.

Every chair is taken. People stand along the back wall with their arms folded, steam rising off their shoulders. Somebody has brought a thermos and is pouring tea into its lid with great care.

On the stage, behind a card that says CHAIR, sits COUNCILLOR EDIE MARSH (60s, reading glasses on a chain, a voice built for weather warnings). She taps the microphone. It howls. Everybody winces.

To her left, at a card that says SECRETARY, sits TOM PELL (30s), taking minutes in a notebook that already has three pages of doodles in it.

EDIE
Order. Order, please. I know it is late and I know it is raining and I know some of you came here straight from the pub.

A cheer from the back. Edie ignores it.

EDIE (CONT'D)
One item on the agenda tonight. The lighthouse.

A murmur runs through the room like wind through a hedge.

TOM
(reading from his notes)
The motion is to sell the lighthouse to the gentleman from the city for one point two million pounds.

At the back, a tall, soaked woman pushes off the wall: NELL ASHBY (40s), the last lighthouse keeper on this coast, though the light has been automatic for twenty years.

NELL
Can I say something?

EDIE
You can say something in the time set aside for public comment, Nell, which is three minutes, which Tom is timing.

Tom holds up a kitchen timer shaped like an egg. He twists it. It ticks.

NELL
My grandfather kept that light. His father kept it before him. Every night for a hundred and forty years somebody from my family climbed those stairs, all one hundred and twelve of them, and lit the lamp, and trimmed it, and sat up with it through gales that took the roofs off half this town. Nobody paid us much. Nobody thanked us at all. And when the ships stopped needing us, we kept climbing anyway, because a light that nobody keeps is just a machine, and a machine doesn't care who it saves. You want to sell it to a man who will put a hot tub on the gallery and rent it to people who have never once been afraid of the sea. Fine. Sell it. But do not stand up here and tell me it is only a building. It is the reason half of you have grandparents at all. It is the reason your boats came home. It is the last thing on this coast that ever stayed awake for anybody, and you are going to let it go for a number that will be spent on a roundabout by Christmas.

The egg timer rings. Nobody moves to stop it. It rings and rings until Tom, embarrassed, smothers it under his notebook.

EDIE
(quietly)
Thank you, Nell.

People begin to file out.

Tom closes his notebook.

Edie takes off her glasses and rubs her eyes.

The thermos man screws the lid back on his thermos.

EXT. TOWN HALL - CONTINUOUS

Rain. Nell comes out alone and stands under the porch light, breathing hard. Across the bay, the lighthouse turns its slow white eye across the water, once, twice, three times.

She watches it until the door opens behind her.

> THE END <
